  <page>
    <title>Wedge-tailed Eagle</title>
    <id>199882</id>
    <revision>
      <id>38562549</id>
      <timestamp>2006-02-07T03:14:44Z</timestamp>
      <contributor>
        <username>Singkong2005</username>
        <id>386359</id>
      </contributor>
      <comment>[[Category:Birds of Australia]]</comment>
      <text xml:space="preserve">{{Taxobox
| color = pink
| name = Wedge-tailed Eagle
| status = {{StatusLeastConcern}}
| image = Wedge-tailed Eagle.jpg
| image_width = 200px
| regnum = [[Animal]]ia
| phylum = [[Chordate|Chordata]]
| classis = [[bird|Aves]]
| ordo = [[Falconiformes]]
| familia = [[Accipitridae]]
| genus = ''[[Aquila (genus)|Aquila]]''
| species = '''''A. audax'''''
| binomial = ''Aquila audax''
| binomial_authority = ([[John Latham (ornithologist)|Latham]], 1802)
}}

The '''Wedge-tailed Eagle''' or '''Arrawa''' (''Aquila audax'') is a very large [[Australasia]]n [[Bird of prey|raptor]] and the most common of all the world's large [[eagle]]s. It has long, fairly broad wings, fully feathered legs, and an unmistakable wedge-shaped tail. Both because of its size&amp;#8212;it is one of the largest birds in the world&amp;#8212;and because of the pointed tail, it can be identified at a glance as a &quot;Wedgie&quot; even by the non-expert. 

As with many raptors, the female is much larger than the male, averaging around 4.2 kg and sometimes over 5 kg. Males are typically around 3.2 kg. Length varies between 0.9 and 1.1 metres, wingspan from 1.8 to 2.5 metres.  

Young Wedge-tailed Eagles are a mid-brown colour with slightly lighter, reddish-brown wings and heads and wings. As they grow older, they become gradually darker, reaching a dark blackish-brown shade after about ten years. Adult females tend to be slightly paler than males.

Wedge-tails are found throughout [[Australia]] and southern [[New Guinea]] in almost all habitats, though they tend to be more common in lightly timbered and open country in southern and eastern Australia. 

They are highly aerial, soaring for hours on end without wingbeat or effort, regularly reaching 6000 feet (2000 m) and sometimes considerably higher. The purpose of this very high flight is unknown.  Their keen eyesight extends into the [[infrared]] and [[ultraviolet]] bands.  This helps them spot prey and allows them to see rising [[thermals]], which they can use to gain altitude while expending little energy.

Most prey is captured on the ground in gliding attacks or (less frequently) in the air. Choice of prey is very much a matter of convenience and opportunity: since the arrival of Europeans, the introduced [[Rabbit (Australia)|rabbit]] and [[Brown Hare]] have become the primary items of the eagle's diet in many areas. The Wedge-tails can eat almost anything of a suitable size, live-caught or as [[carrion]].  

They display considerable adaptability, and have sometimes been known to team up to hunt animals as large as the [[Red Kangaroo]]; to cause [[goat]]s to fall off steep hillsides and injure themselves; or to drive flocks of [[sheep]] or [[kangaroo]]s to isolate a weaker animal.  

Carrion is a major diet item also: Wedge-tails are able to spot the activity of [[raven]]s around a carcass from a great distance, and glide down to appropriate it.

The [[Tasmania]]n sub-species is currently considered threatened and was once subject to a bounty as it was believed to have predated on livestock.

[[Category:Eagles|Raptors]]
[[Category:Birds of Australia]]

[[nl:Wigstaartarend]]</text>
    </revision>
