  <page>
    <title>Richard Boyle, 1st Earl of Burlington</title>
    <id>199891</id>
    <revision>
      <id>27644846</id>
      <timestamp>2005-11-07T19:55:11Z</timestamp>
      <contributor>
        <username>Proteus</username>
        <id>21902</id>
      </contributor>
      <minor />
      <comment>Richard Boyle, 1st Earl of Burlington &amp; 2nd Earl of Cork moved to Richard Boyle, 1st Earl of Burlington</comment>
      <text xml:space="preserve">'''Richard Boyle, 1st Earl of Burlington and 2nd Earl of Cork''' ([[1612]] - [[1698]]) was the second son and sixth child of [[Richard Boyle, 1st Earl of Cork]] and his second wife, formerly [[Catherine Boyle, Countess of Cork|Catherine Fenton]].

==Life Account==

He was born on [[20 October]] [[1612]] at The College in [[Youghal]]. He was [[knight]]ed on [[13 August]] [[1624]], at the his father's house in Youghal, by [[Henry Cary, 1st Viscount Falkland|Lord Falkland]], the [[Lord Lieutenant of Ireland|Lord Deputy of Ireland]]. Upon the death of his brother [[Lewis Boyle, 1st Viscount Boyle of Kinalmeaky|Lord Boyle of Kinalmeaky]] on [[2 September]] [[1642]], he succeeded as 2nd Viscount Boyle of Kinalmeaky.

Richard held principal command at the [[Battle of Liscarroll]] on [[3 September]] [[1642]]. He succeeded as 2nd Earl of Cork on the death of his father on [[15 September]] [[1643]].

[[Charles I of England|King Charles I]] created Lord Cork '''Baron Clifford of Lanesborough''', in the County of York, on [[4 November]] [[1644]], and he was appointed Lord High Treasurer of Ireland on [[16 November]] [[1660]]. [[Charles II of England|King Charles II]] created him '''Earl of Burlington''' on [[20 March]] [[1664]].

In 1689 he was [[attainder|attainted]] by the Irish Parliament which had been assembled by [[James II of England|King James II]].

==Family and Death==

At the age of 22 he married the 21-year-old [[Elizabeth Boyle, Countess of Burlington|Lady Elizabeth Clifford]], daughter of [[Henry Clifford, 5th Earl of Cumberland]], on [[5 July]] [[1635]] in [[Skipton Castle]].

The first of their six children was [[Charles Boyle, Viscount Dungarvan]] (1639-1694) who was born in Youghal on [[12 September]] [[1639]].

His second son was Richard Boyle, who died on [[3 June]] [[1665]] at the battle of [[Solebay]].

Lord and Lady Burlington had four daughters: Frances, Elizabeth, Mary Anne and Henrietta.

When Lord Burlington died, on [[6 January]] [[1698]], he was succeeded by his grandson (the son of his eldest son), [[Charles Boyle, 2nd Earl of Burlington|Charles Boyle, Viscount Dungarvan]].

He was buried on [[3 February]] [[1698]] at [[Londesborough]] in [[Yorkshire]].

