  <page>
    <title>Margery Allingham</title>
    <id>199922</id>
    <revision>
      <id>39235925</id>
      <timestamp>2006-02-11T19:22:02Z</timestamp>
      <contributor>
        <username>Deb</username>
        <id>1219</id>
      </contributor>
      <minor />
      <comment>cat</comment>
      <text xml:space="preserve">'''Margery Louise Allingham''' ([[1904]]-[[1966]]) was born in [[London]] and attended The Perse High School for Girls in [[Cambridge]], before returning to London and the Polytechnic for Speech-Training. Her [[father]] was the [[author]], [[H. J. Allingham]], editor of the ''[[Christian Globe]]'' and ''[[The New London Journal]]'', to which she contributed articles and [[Sexton Blake]] stories.

Her first novel, ''Blackkerchief Dick'', was published in [[1923]] at age 19.  It was allegedly based on a story she heard during a séance, though later in life this was debunked by her husband.  Nevertheless, Allingham continued to have a thread of the [[occult]] in many novels.  ''Blackkerchief Dick'' was well received, but was not a financial success.

Her first work of [[detective fiction]] was a serialized story published by the [[Daily Express]] in [[1927]].  Entitled ''The White Cottage Mystery'', the themes were atypical for a woman writer of the era.  

In [[1928]], she married Philip Youngman Carter, who collaborated with her and designed the jackets for many of her books. They lived on the edge of the Essex Marshes. 

Her breakthrough occurred in [[1929]] with the publication of ''[[The Crime at Black Dudley]]''.  This introduced [[Albert Campion]], albeit originally as a minor character.  Campion proved so popular and successful that Allingham made him the centerpiece of another 16 novels and over 20 short stories, continuing into the 1960's.

Campion is a mysterious upper-class character (played on television by [[Peter Davison]]) that floats between the upper echelons of nobility and government and the shady world of England's criminal class.

Allingham continued to write in other forms, publishing plays, novellas, and dozens of non-Campion short stories, but these are largely ignored today.  

She died in [[1966]] of cancer.  Her final Campion novel, ''[[A Cargo of Eagles]]'' was completed by her husband and published in [[1968]].  Other compilations of her work, both with and without Albert Campion, continued to be released until the 1970's.




&lt;!--  Please add non-Campion stories here if you know them. --&gt;

==External link==
*[http://www.margeryallingham.org.uk/index.html The Margery Allingham Society]

[[Category:1904 births|Allingham, Margery]]
[[Category:1966 deaths|Allingham, Margery]]
[[Category:British crime writers|Allingham, Margery]]
[[Category:English mystery writers|Allingham, Margery]]

[[de:Margery Allingham]]
[[ja:&amp;#12510;&amp;#12540;&amp;#12472;&amp;#12455;&amp;#12522;&amp;#12540;&amp;#12539;&amp;#12450;&amp;#12522;&amp;#12531;&amp;#12460;&amp;#12512;]]
[[lb:Margery Allingham]]</text>
