      <contributor>
        <username>Michael Snow</username>
        <id>34289</id>
      </contributor>
      <minor />
      <text xml:space="preserve">{{Album infobox |
  Name        = Catch a Fire |
  Type        = [[Album (music)|Album]] |
  Artist      = [[The Wailers (reggae)|The Wailers]] |
  Cover       = BobMarleyCatchAFire.jpg | 
  Background  = Orange |
  Released    = [[April 13]], [[1973]] |
  Recorded    = Dynamic Sound Studios, [[Harry J|Harry J. Studios]] and Randy's Studios, [[Kingston, Jamaica|Kingston]], [[Jamaica]] and Island Studios, [[London]], [[England]], 1972&amp;ndash;1973 |
  Genre       = [[Reggae]] |
  Length      = 38:46 |
  Label       = [[Tuff Gong]]/[[Island Records|Island]] |
  Producer    = ??? |
  Reviews     = &lt;nowiki&gt;&lt;/nowiki&gt;
* [[All Music Guide]] (5/5) [http://www.allmusic.com/cg/amg.dll?p=amg&amp;sql=10:2zdqoarayijz link] |
Last album  =  |
This album  = ''Catch a Fire'' &lt;br /&gt; ([[1973]]) |
Next album  = ''[[Burnin']]''&lt;br /&gt;([[1973]]) |
}}
[[Image:Bob marley1.jpg|thumb|Reissue cover art]]
'''''Catch a Fire''''' was the [[major label]] debut album for [[Jamaica]]n [[roots reggae]] band [[The Wailers (reggae)|The Wailers]], released on [[Island Records]] in [[1973]] (see [[1973 in music]]). The album established the band as international superstars, and Marley in particular became world-famous, especially in the [[Caribbean]] and [[Africa]], and among immigrants in [[Europe]] and [[African-American]]s in the [[United States]]. The socially aware lyrics and militant tone surprised many listeners, but others were attracted to [[songwriter]]s [[Bob Marley]] and [[Peter Tosh]]'s confrontational subjects and optimistic view of a future free from oppression.

''Catch a Fire'' peaked at #171 and #51 on [[Billboard Music Charts|Billboard]]'s (North America) Pop Albums and Black Albums charts respectively. It is number 123 on ''[[Rolling Stone]]'''s list of the 500 greatest albums of all time.

==Track listing==
===Side one===
#&quot;Concrete Jungle&quot; (Marley) - 4:13
#&quot;Slave Driver&quot; (Marley) - 2:53
#&quot;400 Years&quot; (Marley/Tosh) - 2:45
#&quot;Stop That Train&quot; (Tosh) - 3:55
#&quot;Baby We've Got A Date&quot; (Marley) - 3:57
===Side two===
#&quot;Stir It Up&quot; (Marley) - 5:32
#&quot;Kinky Reggae&quot; (Marley) - 3:37
#&quot;No More Trouble&quot; (Marley) - 3:57
#&quot;Midnight Ravers&quot; (Marley) - 5:10


== Iconic Cover Art ==
The first 20,000 copies of the original 1973 vinyl release were encased in the famous Zippo sleeve (pictured above).  This sleeve worked as a Zippo lighter case would, opening at a side hinge to reveal the record within.

However, the producers soon realized that this operation would require hand-manufacture, as machinery available was not sufficient to rivet the upper and lower halves of the sleeve together.

Copies of the record from these original pressings have since become moderately collectable, and subsequent pressings are recognizable by their alternative cover art (pictured).

==Personnel==
*Bob Marley	 - 	[[Guitar]] (Acoustic), Guitar, [[Vocals]], [[Record producer|Producer]]
*Aston Barrett	 - 	[[Bass Guitar]]
*Carlton &quot;Carlie&quot; Barrett	 - 	[[Drums]]
*Chris Blackwell	 - 	[[Record producer|Producer]]
*Carlton Lee	 - 	[[Audio engineer|Engineer]]
*Bunny Wailer	 - 	[[Bongos]], [[Conga]], [[Vocals]]
*Peter Tosh	 - 	[[organ (music)|Organ]], [[Guitar]], [[Piano]], [[Vocals]]
*Tony Platt	 - 	[[Audio engineer|Engineer]]
*Bob Weiner	 - 	Design
*Rod Dyer	 - 	Design

[[Category:Bob Marley albums]]
[[Category:1973 albums]]

[[de:Catch a Fire]]
[[fr:Catch A Fire (album)]]
[[it:Catch a Fire]]
[[hu:Catch a Fire]]
[[sv:Catch a Fire]]</text>
