  <page>
    <title>British Ten Pence coin</title>
    <id>200093</id>
    <revision>
      <id>36836103</id>
      <timestamp>2006-01-26T20:22:57Z</timestamp>
      <contributor>
        <username>Kbdank71</username>
        <id>197953</id>
      </contributor>
      <comment>[[WP:AWB|AWB assisted]] per [[WP:CFD]] [[Wikipedia:Categories for deletion/Log/2006 January 15|Jan 15]]</comment>
      <text xml:space="preserve">[[Image:10p-front.jpg|right|150px|Reverse]][[Image:10p-2001.jpg|right|150px|2001 obverse]]
The [[United Kingdom|British]] decimal '''Ten Pence (10p)''' coin was issued in [[1968]] in preparation for the forthcoming [[Decimal Day|decimalisation]] of the coinage. As at that time it had the same value, size, and weight as the pre-existing [[British coin Florin|Florin]] or Two Shillings coin it may be viewed as a continuation of the older coin, which remained in circulation.

The coin is minted from an [[alloy]] of 75% [[copper]] and 25% [[nickel]]. The 1962 version of the coin weighed 8.31 [[1 E-2 kg|grams]] and had a diameter of 28.50 [[1 E-2 m|millimetres]]. In [[1992]] a smaller version weighing 6.50 grams and with a diameter of 24.50 millimetres was introduced, and all the older 10p and florin coins were withdrawn from circulation and demonetised from [[1 July]] [[1993]].  This reform was done a year after a similar reform of the [[British coin Five Pence|Five Pence]] coin, and the new Ten Pence was only a gram heavier and half a millimetre larger than the previously withdrawn Five Pence.

The reverse of the coin, designed by [[Christopher Ironside]], is a crowned lion (formally, ''Part of the crest of England, a lion passant guardant royally crowned''), with the numeral &quot;10&quot; below the lion, and either &lt;small&gt;NEW PENCE&lt;/small&gt; ([[1968]]&amp;ndash;[[1981]]) or &lt;small&gt;TEN PENCE&lt;/small&gt; ([[1982]]&amp;ndash;date) above the lion. 

During the history of the coin, three different obverses have been used so far&amp;mdash;between 1968 and [[1984]] the head of Queen [[Elizabeth II of the United Kingdom|Elizabeth II]] by [[Arnold Machin]] was used, between 1985 and 1997 the head by [[Raphael Maklouf]] was used, and since [[1998]] one by [[Ian Rank-Broadley]] has been used. In all cases, the inscription used is &lt;small&gt;ELIZABETH II [[British coinage#D.G.REG.F.D.|D.G.REG.F.D.]] date&lt;/small&gt;.

== External links ==
[http://www.royalmint.com/RoyalMint/web/site/Corporate/Corp_british_coinage/CoinDesign/10pCoin.asp British Royal Mint - 10p Coin]

{{British_coinage}}

[[Category:Coins of the United Kingdom|Ten Pence]]</text>
    </revision>
